.TH BIFOP 1 "October 2026" "BIDeT 4D" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bifop \- change a BIF (BIDeT Intermediate Format): weight, colour, shape, 3D, animation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bifop
[\fB\-i\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIFILE\fR] \fIOP\fR [\fIOP\fR ...]
.br
.B bifop \-\-list
.br
.B bifin
\&... |
.B bifop
.IR OP " [" OP " ...] |"
.B bifout
\&...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bifop
is the second tool of BIDeT 4D.  It reads a BIF from standard input (or
.BR \-i ),
applies the operations in the order given, and writes the changed BIF to standard output (or
.BR \-o ).
What an operation does not know about passes through untouched (unknown layers, array names, keys and chunks), the credit
and licence are kept, and each operation is appended to the picture's history, as BIF\-SPEC.md asks of a manipulator.
.SH OPERATIONS: SYNTAX
An operation is
.IR name ,
.IB name : value
(the value of its first parameter), or
.IB name : key = value , key = value \fR.
Lists inside a value are joined with
.B +
(\fBlayer=strokes+text\fR).  Colours are
.B #rrggbb
or a name.  Operations that take
.B layer=
change only those layer ids (default: every layer they apply to);
.B frame=
only that frame.  Several operations can be given in one command:
.B bifop pen:1.5 theme:ink=#00ff66,paper=#101820 crop:margin=8
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-i " FILE" "\fR, " \-\-input " FILE"
The BIF to change (default: standard input).
.TP
.BI \-o " FILE" "\fR, " \-\-output " FILE"
Write here (default: standard output).
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
Describe every operation and its parameters, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-truncated
Work on what arrived of a file that ends early.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH OPERATIONS
.SS Style
.TP
.BI pen: factor
Multiply the stroke width of vector layers.  Letters (filled outlines) get bolder by an outline of (factor \- 1) stems; they
cannot be made thinner than the font drew them.  Also
.BR layer ", " frame .
.TP
.BI theme: ink = C , paper = C , text = C , accent = C
Re-colour by role: every palette entry with that role gets the colour.  A
.B paper
also becomes the background.
.TP
.BI recolor: FROM = TO + ...
Change palette colours (the parameter is
.BR map ,
given bare, as here, or as
.BR map=... ).
.I FROM
is a palette index or a
.BR #rrggbb ,
.I TO
a colour:
.B recolor:2=#ff0000
or
.BR recolor:#cc2828=#00aa00+3=blue .
.TP
.BI opacity: value
The opacity of layers, 0 to 1.  Also
.BR layer ", " frame .
.TP
.BI blend: mode
How a layer combines with what is below it:
.BR normal ", " multiply ", " screen " or " erase .
Also
.BR layer ", " frame .
.SS Layers and frames
.TP
.BI keep: layer " \fR, " drop: layer " \fR, " hide: layer " \fR, " show: layer
Keep only these layers; remove them; keep them but do not draw them; draw them (again).  Layer ids are joined with
.BR + .
.TP
.BI frame: n
Keep frame
.I n
of an animation, as a still picture.
.TP
.BI meta: title = T , comment = T , author = T
Set descriptive metadata.
.SS Geometry
.TP
.BI crop: margin = M , box = x0 + y0 + x1 + y1
Cut the canvas down to what is drawn plus a margin (canvas units), or to a box.  The edges fall on whole units, so a pixel
picture is not resampled.  An animation is cut to what any of its frames draws, so that the frames stay the same size.
.TP
.BI scale: factor
Change the units: the canvas and everything on it grow by the factor, vectors without loss.
.TP
.BI stretch: y = F , x = F
Stretch along one axis, as WordArt does for tall or squat letters.
.TP
.BI rotate: degrees
Turn the picture clockwise; the canvas grows to hold it.
.TP
.BI flip: axis
Mirror it:
.B h
(left to right, the default) or
.B v
(top to bottom).
.TP
.BI skew: x = D , y = D
Shear: lean the verticals to the right by D degrees (italic), tilt the baseline downwards to the right by D degrees.
.PP
Operations that break the character grid (\fBstretch\fR, \fBrotate\fR, \fBflip\fR, \fBskew\fR and the warps below) forget the
grid and the hidden
.B cells
layer.
.SS Shapes: WordArt warps (vector layers; a raster layer is skipped, with a warning)
.TP
.BI arc: bend = F
Bow shapes into an arch: how far the middle rises, as a fraction of the picture height (default 0.4; negative: a bowl).
.TP
.BI squeeze: peak = F , ends = F
Taller in the middle than at the ends (defaults 1.4 and 0.8).
.TP
.BI wave: amplitude = F , length = F , phase = F
Ripple up and down: the height as a fraction of the picture height (0.1), the wave length as a fraction of its width (1.0),
where it starts, in turns (0).  Strokes are made finer first so that they bend.
.PP
The warps also take
.B step
(the longest segment before warping, in canvas units),
.B layer
and
.BR frame .
.TP
.BI simplify: tolerance
Fewer vertices: drop points of vector paths that lie within the tolerance of the line between their neighbours.  Also
.BR layer ", " frame .
.SS 3D and animation
These take ONE flat frame, draw it once, and replace it with raster frames: a lit solid on a shadow on the screen.  They are the
animations of bidet3d, and the new one, in which the picture lies flat on the screen and pops out of it.
.TP
.B extrude
A still: the picture extruded to
.BR depth ,
turned to
.BR view ,
lit, on its shadow.
.TP
.B spin
Turns about the vertical axis, a seamless loop.  Also
.B speed
(degrees a second, default 90; negative: the other way) and
.B turns
(default 1).
.TP
.B sway
Swings to and fro, a seamless loop.  Also
.B degrees
(each side of the view, default 25) and
.B time
(seconds for there and back, default 4).
.TP
.B popout
2D to 3D: the picture flat on the screen, then it grows its depth, rises toward the viewer on a shadow that parts from it, and
turns to the view.  The first frame is the flat picture.  Also
.B time
(default 1.6 seconds).
.TP
.BI motion: path = moves
Several moves, each from where the last stopped, joined with
.BR + :
.BR flat ", " tick ", " pop ", " hold ", " spin ", " sway ", " still .
.B flat+tick+pop+hold+spin
starts as the picture is, lets a ticker or caption be laid over a run of its frames, pops out, rests, and turns.  The
parameters of the moves:
.BR time " (a pop, 1.6 s), " hold " (1 s), " flat " (1 s), " ticks " (24), " tickms " (40), " speed ", " turns ", " degrees ,
and
.B swing
(a sway, 4 s).
.PP
The parameters of all of them:
.TP
.BI depth = F
Extrusion depth, as a fraction of the drawing's shorter side (default 0.22).
.TP
.BI hover = F
How far it floats off the screen when it is up, in the same unit (default 0.6; 0 for
.BR extrude ).
.TP
.BI view = yaw + pitch + roll
The camera in degrees (default \-20+8+0); the yaw alone is allowed.
.TP
.BI tall = F
Stretch the height before extruding (WordArt's taller letters, 1.4).  A later setting replaces an earlier one.
.TP
.BI material = NAME
The face:
.BR rainbow ", " chrome ", " gold ", " superhero ", " sunset ", " aqua ", " blues ", " purple ", " slate ", " lime ", "
.BR cornflower ", " graphite ", " brick ", " cobalt ", " horizon ,
or
.BR #rrggbb ;
default: the picture's own colours.
.TP
.BI side = back + front
The colours of the extrusion, for example
.BR #2a1040+#5a2a7a .
.TP
.BI bevel = F ", " perspective = F ", " shadow = F
The edge lighting of the face, 0 to 1 (the material's); the camera distance in picture widths, smaller meaning stronger
perspective (2.5); the strength of the shadow, 0 for none (1).  A black shadow does not show on a black page.
.TP
.BI fps = N ", " res = N
Frames a second (15); the pixels per canvas unit that the frames are drawn at (2;
.B bifout \-\-scale 1
averages them down).
.SS Scrolling text
.TP
.BI scroll: dir
Scrolling text: the picture slides through a window, round and round, a seamless loop of frames (the idea of the CP/M LARGE
letter program: a line of big letters slid across the screen until a key was pressed).  It takes ONE flat picture, so do
.B crop
and any warp or 3D look first; the canvas becomes the window.
.B dir
is
.BR left " (a ticker, the default), " right ", " up " (rolling credits) or " down ;
right and down run the lap the other way.  The parameters:
.B window
(how much shows at once, in times the picture's size across the way it travels: its height going sideways, its width going up
or down; 5),
.B speed
(in that unit a second: 3 sideways, 0.2 up or down),
.B time
(seconds for one lap, instead of a speed: the loop lasts exactly this long),
.B gap
(empty space between the end of one lap and the start of the next, in the same unit; default the window, so the picture has left
before it comes back, as on CP/M),
.B start
.RB ( rest :
begins with the start of the picture in the window;
.BR enter :
begins empty and the picture comes in at the far edge) and
.B fps
(25).  A vector layer is cut to what shows in each frame (compound letters whole), so a long banner costs no more per frame
than a short one; a raster layer, such as an extruded still, is moved whole.  Anything that sits on the character grid is forgotten.
.SS Joining pictures
.TP
.BI join: file
Play the other picture(s) after this one: their frames are added, the colours merged, and the canvas is the larger of the two
(the smaller is centred).  Several files:
.BR a.bif+b.bif .
.TP
.BI overlay: file , first = N , last = N , x = D , y = D
Draw the other picture's layers on top of frames of this one (colours merged).  A one\-frame picture goes on every frame it
covers (default all); a longer one goes frame by frame from
.BR first .
Negative frame numbers count from the end.  For captions, tickers and logos.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
bifin art.txt | bifop pen:2 theme:ink=#00ff66,paper=#101820 | bifout \-s
bifop \-i art.bif \-o thick.bif pen:1.5
echo Hello | bifin \-f lettering | bifop arc:bend=0.4 extrude:material=gold | bifout \-o hello.png
echo Hello | bifin \-f lettering | bifop popout:material=rainbow | bifout \-o hello.gif
echo Hello | bifin \-f lettering | bifop motion:path=pop+hold+spin,hold=2.4 | bifout \-s
echo Hello World | bifin \-f lettering | bifop crop:margin=10 scroll:left,start=enter | bifout \-o ticker.gif
bifop \-i a.bif join:b.bif overlay:caption.bif,first=0,last=0 \-o both.bif
.fi
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
An unknown operation or parameter, a value that is not what the parameter takes, or the result not being a valid BIF, is
reported on standard error and ends the run with a non-zero status.  A 3D operation, or a scroll, on a picture of several frames is an
error: do all the moves in one
.BR motion ,
and scroll last.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bifin (1),
.BR bifout (1),
.BR bidet (1).
.B bifop \-\-list
describes every operation and its defaults; the format is in BIF\-SPEC.md.
